    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 8th with a potent offensive outlook, anchored by the elite 77.33 efficiency rating of **Trea Turner**. Facing the right-handed Dollander, **Trea Turner** is our strongest play today; his combination of leadoff duties and a high-yield matchup against a pitcher with a slow delivery provides an immediate boost to our stolen base probability. Similarly, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (71.93) is a high-upside start despite hitting 8th, as he benefits from a 14% platoon adjustment and an ideal matchup against Rocker\u2019s slow-to-the-plate profile, effectively prioritizing speed in our scarcest category. **Kyle Schwarber** (70.60) rounds out our top-tier tier of starters, riding an impressive 0.394 xwOBA into a favorable park environment.\n\nWe must remain cautious with our middle-of-the-order power threats. While **Yordan Alvarez** (54.97) remains a threat due to his elite 0.425 xwOBA, his score is tempered by a difficult lefty-on-lefty matchup against Lodolo, which suppresses his ceiling. Similarly, **Kazuma Okamoto** (47.46) and **Junior Caminero** (55.41) face significant SP skill headwinds against Detmers and Early, respectively, though both gain some traction from favorable park factors. The most difficult bench decision involved **Hyeseong Kim** (36.38); while he was considered for the utility slot, his projected matchup against the elite Chris Sale (xERA 2.94) combined with an assumed bottom-of-the-order placement rendered him a \"below floor\" option, forcing the optimizer to prioritize the higher-upside platoon splits of our active starters. Note that while **Gary Sanchez** carries a strong score of 51.15, his inclusion relies on a \"Lineup Pending\" assumption at the #3 hole; should he be shifted down the order, his efficiency may erode, though we remain committed to this configuration given the scarcity of high-tier catching production.",
